--[[
Builds ordinary Lua values straight out of parsed bytes.

A parser written against this module never builds a table itself. It says what
it found -- an array opens here, this byte range is a key, that one is a number
-- and the module builds the value behind it.

```nupp
local u32 = math.u32
local valuebuilder = serde.valuebuilder

--- Decodes the fixed document `{"id": 41}`.
--- @raises string when the stream is left incomplete
@aot
local function decode(source: string, nullValue: any): any
    local builder = valuebuilder.new(nullValue)

    valuebuilder.openobject(builder, u32.wrap(1))
    valuebuilder.key(builder, source, u32.wrap(2), u32.wrap(2), false)
    valuebuilder.numberslice(builder, source, u32.wrap(7), u32.wrap(2))
    valuebuilder.close(builder)

    return valuebuilder.finish(builder)
end
```

Values are reported as the parser reaches them, so there is no intermediate
representation. Every offset, length, and index here counts from zero, because
they address the parser's bytes rather than a Lua string. Each is a `uint32`,
which an `@aot` body establishes with `math.u32.wrap` rather than by
passing a number literal.

The lowering is why the module exists. Inside an `@aot` function these calls
become one native construction pass: tables are presized from the capacities the
parser authored, every unfinished container stays rooted in a Lua stack slot
across allocations, writes go through the raw-set API so the barriers stay
correct, and each string is copied once into Lua-owned storage. Outside `@aot`
the same calls run as plain Lua, which is the `aot = "off"` oracle the lowering
is checked against -- so one parser is portable across both.

It is the one construction boundary an AOT builder may cross, and a narrow one:
no `lua_State`, no stack index, and no collector object appears in a signature
here. A stream handle and a scratch buffer are local construction state under
AOT, so neither can be returned, reassigned, stored in a table, or handed to an
ordinary call.

See [ahead-of-time.md](docs/learn/performance/ahead-of-time/index.md) for what
generated code is allowed to do.
]]
module serde.valuebuilder

local implementation = require("nupp.serde.internal.valuebuilder")

--- Starts a direct stream of values.
---
--- This is the streaming shape. Open a container with
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.openarray) or [](serde.valuebuilder.openobject), add
--- values to it, [](serde.valuebuilder.close) it, and take the single root back
--- from [](serde.valuebuilder.finish). A stream must publish exactly one root:
--- adding a second value at the top level raises, and so does finishing with
--- none.
---
--- The ordinary implementation keeps growable Lua state and is the `aot = "off"`
--- oracle. VM-aware AOT keeps the same state in a bounded C stack object and
--- roots every unfinished container on the Lua stack; it admits at most 1,024
--- open containers. Use [](serde.valuebuilder.newsized) to choose that bound, and
--- to reserve scratch for transformed strings, when the parser knows better.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local u32 = math.u32
--- local valuebuilder = serde.valuebuilder
---
--- --- Builds `[true, null]` without a source document.
--- --- @raises string when the stream is left incomplete
--- @aot
--- local function pair(nullValue: any): any
---     local builder = valuebuilder.new(nullValue)
---
---     valuebuilder.openarray(builder, u32.wrap(2))
---     valuebuilder.bool(builder, true)
---     valuebuilder.null(builder)
---     valuebuilder.close(builder)
---
---     return valuebuilder.finish(builder)
--- end
--- ```
---
--- @param nullvalue what [](serde.valuebuilder.null) adds
--- @return the stream handle, which under AOT is local construction state and
---     cannot be returned, reassigned, stored, or passed to an ordinary call
export const new = implementation.new

--- Starts a stream with authored bounds for native frame and transformed-byte
--- scratch storage.
---
--- Same contract as [](serde.valuebuilder.new); only the storage differs. The
--- ordinary implementation keeps normal growable Lua state and ignores both
--- bounds, because plain Lua has nothing to preallocate. AOT uses them to
--- allocate two rooted regions once: the first 16 frames stay inline and deeper
--- streams lazily spill to Lua-rooted storage, and the byte region is allocated
--- on the first escaped string and then reused. Publication still copies exactly
--- once into a normal Lua string.
---
--- Reach for this over [](serde.valuebuilder.new) when the document may nest
--- deeper than 1,024 containers, or when the parser already knows the longest
--- string it can produce and would rather not grow into it.
--- @param nullvalue what [](serde.valuebuilder.null) adds
--- @param maxdepth the most containers that may be open at once
--- @param stringcapacity the most bytes one transformed string may need
--- @return the stream handle, on the same terms as [](serde.valuebuilder.new)
export const newsized = implementation.newSized

--- Publishes an integer magnitude accumulated in native uint64 arithmetic.
--- Internal to AOT codecs that must avoid a decimal rescan.
--- @raises string outside an AOT-compiled function
export const integer64 = implementation.integer64

--- Publishes a decimal from the uint64 mantissa and base-ten exponent already
--- accumulated by a codec. The native fast path handles Clinger's exact range;
--- other inputs fall back to the authored source slice for correct rounding.
--- @raises string outside an AOT-compiled function or when the source range is invalid
export const decimal64 = implementation.decimal64

--- Returns the rooted string's byte length without making a substring.
---
--- This, [](serde.valuebuilder.byte), and [](serde.valuebuilder.word)
--- are the three readers that let an AOT parser read its input where it already
--- lies. `#bytes` returns the same in ordinary Lua; going through here is what
--- keeps the read admissible inside `@aot`.
--- @param bytes the rooted string to measure
--- @return its length in bytes
export const len = implementation.length

--- Views the rooted bytes as a byte span, which is what the vector loads read.
---
--- The general `simd` algebra loads from a `Span<uint8>`, and a
--- value-building AOT entry has no way to be handed one: its parameters cross
--- the Lua stack, where a pointer and a count are not values. This returns the
--- span over the bytes the entry already roots, so `species:load` reads the
--- input where it lies rather than through a byte-specialized counterpart.
---
--- Inside `@aot` the span is the parameter's own pointer and length, so the
--- call costs nothing and a `cursor + species.lanes <= length(bytes)` guard
--- proves a load through it exactly as the same guard proves one through a
--- span parameter. It must name a parameter directly; a local holding a copy
--- of one is refused, because the C name the load addresses is the
--- parameter's.
---
--- In ordinary Lua the span is the same checked view `binary.span.fromstring`
--- returns, and the bytes have to be a string: a view roots nothing itself, so
--- there is no rooted string for a buffer's unread bytes to be viewed through.
--- Every vector path is inside a `simd.species` guard, which is nil there, so
--- an interpreted body never reaches this.
--- @param bytes the rooted string or buffer to view
--- @return the byte view over it
--- @raises string when the bytes are not a string outside an AOT entry
export const bytes = implementation.bytes

--- Reads a byte at a zero-based offset.
---
--- The scanning counterpart of [](serde.valuebuilder.len); read whole words
--- with [](serde.valuebuilder.word) when the bytes are a packed side table rather
--- than the document.
--- @param bytes the rooted string to read
--- @param offset the zero-based byte offset
--- @return the byte, 0 through 255
--- @raises string when offset is outside bytes
export const byte = implementation.byte

--- Reads a byte under a dominating `offset < length(bytes)` proof.
---
--- Ordinary Lua performs the same checked read as [](serde.valuebuilder.byte).
--- In an AOT entry the verifier requires this call to be inside the true arm or
--- body of that exact bounds check, then emits a direct rooted-string load.
--- Reach for it in a parser's established cursor loop; use `byte` everywhere
--- else.
--- @param bytes the rooted string to read
--- @param offset the proved in-bounds zero-based byte offset
--- @return the byte, 0 through 255
--- @raises string when offset is outside bytes outside AOT
export const byteat = implementation.byteAt

--- Reads a native-endian uint32 at a zero-based word index.
---
--- This is how a parser reads a packed side table, a structural index or a tape,
--- back out of a rooted string instead of a Lua array of numbers. The word at
--- `index` begins at byte offset `index * 4`.
--- @param bytes the rooted string to read
--- @param index the zero-based word index
--- @return the word's value
--- @raises string when the complete word is outside bytes
export const word = implementation.word

--- Allocates a fixed uint32 work buffer, every word of it readable at once.
---
--- The difference from [](serde.valuebuilder.newwordscratch) is what
--- "initialized" means. That buffer grows: a word is readable once something
--- has written it, so reading ahead of the writes raises, and the bound every
--- access is checked against is a length that moves. This one is zero from the
--- moment it exists, so every word below the capacity is readable and writable
--- immediately and the bound never moves.
---
--- Reach for it where the buffer is a table of a size the source knows -- a
--- message schedule, a state vector, a lookup -- and for the appending one
--- where the size is the input's. The checks are the same and refuse the same
--- indexes; what changes is that a fixed buffer's bound is a number the code
--- generator has rather than a field it must load, which is what lets it
--- discharge the comparison itself in a counted loop. `bench/sha256` measures
--- `nupp.digest.internal.sha256` at 0.849x the hand-written C before and 0.963x after.
---
--- An `@aot` entry requires the capacity to be a literal, since a bound that is
--- not known is not one anything can reason about. Ordinary Nupp takes any.
--- @param capacity how many words this buffer holds
--- @return the scratch handle, local to the call that allocated it
export const newfixedwordscratch = implementation.newFixedWordScratch

--- Allocates a bounded uint32 work buffer local to one decode.
---
--- AOT lowers this to Lua-owned userdata, so its address remains stable across
--- value allocations without crossing the public API as a pointer. Words are
--- initialized by contiguous writes before they can be read: append or overwrite
--- with [](serde.valuebuilder.setscratchword), and read it back with
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.scratchword). For transformed bytes rather than indexes,
--- use [](serde.valuebuilder.newbytescratch).
--- A zero-capacity scratch allocates no userdata and remains valid until an
--- attempted read or write reports the ordinary bounds error.
--- @param capacity the most words this buffer will ever hold
--- @return the scratch handle, local to the call that allocated it
export const newwordscratch = implementation.newWordScratch

--- Reads a word from a local work buffer.
---
--- Only initialized words can be read, so an index at or past the buffer's
--- current length raises rather than answering whatever was there.
--- @param scratch a buffer from [](serde.valuebuilder.newwordscratch)
--- @param index the zero-based word index
--- @return the word's value
--- @raises string when the indexed word has not been initialized
export const scratchword = implementation.scratchWord

--- Returns the initialized word count of a local work buffer.
--- @param scratch a buffer from [](serde.valuebuilder.newwordscratch)
--- @return the number of initialized words
export const scratchlen = implementation.scratchLength

--- Writes an existing word or appends the next contiguous word.
---
--- Writing at the buffer's current length appends and grows it by one; writing
--- below that overwrites. There is no way to write past the end, which is what
--- makes "initialized" mean the same thing as "below the length" for
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.scratchword).
--- @param scratch a buffer from [](serde.valuebuilder.newwordscratch)
--- @param index the zero-based word index, at most the current length
--- @param value the word to store
--- @raises string when the index leaves a gap or exceeds the capacity
export const setscratchword = implementation.setScratchWord

--- Allocates a fixed byte work buffer, every byte of it readable at once.
---
--- The byte counterpart of [](serde.valuebuilder.newfixedwordscratch), and
--- the difference from [](serde.valuebuilder.newbytescratch) is the same
--- one. That buffer grows: a byte is readable once something has written it,
--- so the bound every access is checked against is a length that moves. This
--- one is zero from the moment it exists, so every byte below the capacity is
--- readable and writable immediately, in any order.
---
--- Reach for it where the buffer is a block of a size the source knows -- a
--- digest's final block, a fixed-width field, a lookup -- and for the appending
--- one where the size is the input's. The checks are the same and refuse the
--- same indexes; what changes is that the bound is a number rather than a field
--- to load, and that an `@aot` entry can stand the storage on the C stack
--- instead of allocating it. `bench/sha256` measured those two together at
--- about a hundred nanoseconds of a two-hundred-and-ninety nanosecond digest.
---
--- There is no emptying it: [](serde.valuebuilder.resetbytescratch) refuses
--- a fixed buffer, because a buffer with no fill state has none to discard.
---
--- An `@aot` entry requires the capacity to be a literal, since a bound that is
--- not known is not one anything can reason about. Ordinary Nupp takes any.
--- @param capacity how many bytes this buffer holds
--- @return the scratch handle, local to the call that allocated it
export const newfixedbytescratch = implementation.newFixedByteScratch

--- Allocates a bounded byte work buffer local to one decode.
---
--- This is where a codec assembles bytes that are not a range of the source, an
--- unescaped string or a decoded field, before publishing them. Write with
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.setscratchbyte), read with
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.scratchbyte), empty it between values with
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.resetbytescratch), and publish a finished range with
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.stringscratch) or [](serde.valuebuilder.keyscratch).
---
--- When the bytes are already a contiguous range of the source, skip all of this and
--- use [](serde.valuebuilder.string) or [](serde.valuebuilder.key), which
--- read the source in place.
--- @param capacity the most bytes this buffer will ever hold
--- @return the scratch handle, local to the call that allocated it
export const newbytescratch = implementation.newByteScratch

--- Reads an initialized byte from a local work buffer.
--- @param scratch a buffer from [](serde.valuebuilder.newbytescratch)
--- @param index the zero-based byte index
--- @return the byte, 0 through 255
--- @raises string when the indexed byte has not been initialized
export const scratchbyte = implementation.scratchByte

--- Writes an existing byte or appends the next contiguous byte.
---
--- Appends and grows by one when `index` is the current length, overwrites below
--- it, on the same terms as [](serde.valuebuilder.setscratchword).
--- @param scratch a buffer from [](serde.valuebuilder.newbytescratch)
--- @param index the zero-based byte index, at most the current length
--- @param value the byte to store, 0 through 255
--- @raises string when the write leaves a gap, exceeds capacity, or is not a byte
export const setscratchbyte = implementation.setScratchByte

--- Writes four bytes at once, least significant first.
---
--- The counterpart of [](serde.valuebuilder.word) on the writing side, and the
--- reason it exists: a codec that produces bytes in groups pays one bounds
--- check and one store per group rather than four of each. `bench/base64`
--- measured the byte-at-a-time path at over half of its encode.
---
--- The order is little-endian and stated rather than native, unlike the
--- read: a defined order is one a plain-Lua implementation can honour without
--- knowing the machine, and on every target Nupp has it is what a native store
--- already does. `index` is a byte index, so the four bytes land at `index`
--- through `index + 3`.
---
--- Appends and grows by four when `index` is the current length, overwrites
--- below it, and refuses a write that would straddle the two.
--- @param scratch a buffer from [](serde.valuebuilder.newbytescratch)
--- @param index the zero-based byte index, at most the current length
--- @param value the four bytes, least significant at `index`
--- @raises string when the write leaves a gap, straddles the length, or exceeds
---     capacity
export const setscratchbytes4 = implementation.setScratchBytes4

--- Makes a byte scratch buffer empty without reallocating it.
---
--- Call this between values so that one bounded allocation serves a whole
--- decode. Bytes below the old length remain in memory but are no longer
--- readable, since [](serde.valuebuilder.scratchbyte) admits only initialized
--- indexes.
--- @param scratch a buffer from [](serde.valuebuilder.newbytescratch)
--- @raises string when the buffer is a fixed one, which has no fill state to discard
export const resetbytescratch = implementation.resetByteScratch

--- Publishes initialized scratch bytes as a normal Lua string value.
---
--- The copy into Lua-owned storage happens here, exactly once. Use
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.keyscratch) for the object-key position instead, and
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.string) when the bytes are already a range of the
--- source and need no assembling.
--- @param builder the stream
--- @param scratch a buffer from [](serde.valuebuilder.newbytescratch)
--- @param start the range's zero-based first byte
--- @param len the range's length in bytes
--- @raises string when the range is not initialized
--- @raises string when the value would be a second root, or an object value with no key
export const stringscratch = implementation.stringScratch

--- Publishes initialized scratch bytes as the next object key.
---
--- The key-position counterpart of [](serde.valuebuilder.stringscratch). The very
--- next value added becomes this key's value.
--- @param builder the stream
--- @param scratch a buffer from [](serde.valuebuilder.newbytescratch)
--- @param start the range's zero-based first byte
--- @param len the range's length in bytes
--- @raises string when outside an object, when a key is already pending, or when the
---     range is not initialized
export const keyscratch = implementation.keyScratch

--- Opens an array. Every value added after it belongs to it until the matching
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.close).
---
--- `capacity` is a presizing hint taken from what the parser already counted, not
--- a limit: a wrong guess costs a rehash, never an error.
--- @param builder the stream
--- @param capacity how many elements to presize the array part for
export const openarray = implementation.openArray

--- Opens an object. Each value inside it must be preceded by a key from
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.key) or [](serde.valuebuilder.keyscratch), until the
--- matching [](serde.valuebuilder.close).
---
--- `capacity` presizes the hash part and is a hint, as in
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.openarray).
--- @param builder the stream
--- @param capacity how many entries to presize the hash part for
export const openobject = implementation.openObject

--- Returns how many containers are currently open.
---
--- This and [](serde.valuebuilder.state) let an iterative parser see the
--- construction state; there is deliberately no way to read a value back out
--- of a container it has already been added to.
--- @param builder the stream
--- @return the number of open containers, 0 at the top level
export const depth = implementation.depth

--- Returns the current container's tag: 5 for an array and 6 for an object.
--- @param builder the stream
--- @return the innermost open container's tag
--- @raises string when the stream has no current container
export const kind = implementation.kind

--- Returns the number of complete values in the current container.
---
--- Complete means a value has arrived, so an object with a key pending and no
--- value yet still returns the count before it. A null dropped because the
--- stream has no null replacement is a value that arrived.
--- @param builder the stream
--- @return how many values the innermost open container holds
--- @raises string when the stream has no current container
export const count = implementation.count

--- Returns the current container kind and whether it has a complete value in
--- one query. Zero means no container is open. Otherwise the low byte is 5 for
--- an array or 6 for an object, and bit `0x100` is set after its first value.
---
--- This is the delimiter-loop shape of [](serde.valuebuilder.depth),
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.kind), and [](serde.valuebuilder.count). It
--- avoids making a native parser cross the builder boundary three times when
--- it only needs to distinguish the root, an empty container, and a nonempty
--- container.
--- @param builder the stream
--- @return the packed current state
export const state = implementation.state

--- Sets the next object key from a range of the rooted source.
---
--- A name the object already has is refused, whether the earlier member was stored or
--- was a dropped null. The range is read in place, so a key that holds no escape costs
--- one copy and no substring. Pass `escaped` as true only when the range may hold
--- backslash escapes; false takes the bytes exactly as they lie. Assemble a key that is
--- not a contiguous source range with [](serde.valuebuilder.keyscratch) instead.
--- @param builder the stream
--- @param source the rooted string the range indexes
--- @param start the range's zero-based first byte
--- @param len the range's length in bytes
--- @param escaped whether the range may hold backslash escapes
--- @raises string when outside an object, or when a key is already pending
--- @raises string when the source range is out of bounds
--- @raises string when an escape in the range is malformed
--- @raises string when the object already has a member of this name
export const key = implementation.key

--- Adds a string value from a range of the rooted source.
---
--- The value counterpart of [](serde.valuebuilder.key), on the same terms: the
--- range is read in place, and `escaped` says whether it needs decoding first.
--- @param builder the stream
--- @param source the rooted string the range indexes
--- @param start the range's zero-based first byte
--- @param len the range's length in bytes
--- @param escaped whether the range may hold backslash escapes
--- @raises string when the source range is out of bounds
--- @raises string when an escape in the range is malformed
--- @raises string when the value would be a second root, or an object value with no key
export const string = implementation.string

--- Adds a number the parser has already converted.
---
--- Use [](serde.valuebuilder.numberslice) or
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.integerslice) instead when the number is still a range
--- of the source, so that the conversion happens without a substring.
--- @param builder the stream
--- @param value the number to add
--- @raises string when the value would be a second root, or an object value with no key
export const number = implementation.number

--- Adds a number token from a range of the rooted source, converting it in
--- place.
---
--- This accepts any numeric token. When the token is known to be an integer,
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.integerslice) says so and is cheaper.
--- @param builder the stream
--- @param source the rooted string the range indexes
--- @param start the range's zero-based first byte
--- @param len the range's length in bytes
--- @raises string when the source range is out of bounds
--- @raises string when the source range is not a number token
--- @raises string when the value would be a second root, or an object value with no key
export const numberslice = implementation.numberSlice

--- Adds an integer token from a range of the rooted source.
---
--- The integer counterpart of [](serde.valuebuilder.numberslice). AOT accumulates
--- short integers directly without entering `strtod`, and falls back to the same
--- checked binary64 conversion for longer tokens; the ordinary path retains
--- `tonumber` as the behavioral oracle either way.
--- @param builder the stream
--- @param source the rooted string the range indexes
--- @param start the range's zero-based first byte
--- @param len the range's length in bytes
--- @raises string when the source range is out of bounds
--- @raises string when the source range is not an integer token
--- @raises string when the value would be a second root, or an object value with no key
export const integerslice = implementation.integerSlice

--- Adds a boolean value.
--- @param builder the stream
--- @param value the boolean to add
--- @raises string when the value would be a second root, or an object value with no key
export const bool = implementation.boolean

--- Adds the null replacement the stream was started with by
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.new) or [](serde.valuebuilder.newsized).
--- @param builder the stream
--- @raises string when the value would be a second root, or an object value with no key
export const null = implementation.null

--- Closes the innermost open container and adds it to whatever encloses it.
---
--- Every [](serde.valuebuilder.openarray) and
--- [](serde.valuebuilder.openobject) needs exactly one of these. Closing the
--- outermost container publishes the root, which [](serde.valuebuilder.finish)
--- then returns.
--- @param builder the stream
--- @raises string when no container is open, or when an object key has no value
--- @raises string when the closed container would be a second root
export const close = implementation.close

--- Ends the stream and returns its single root value.
---
--- ```nupp:fragment
--- valuebuilder.openarray(builder, 1)
--- valuebuilder.number(builder, 41)
--- valuebuilder.close(builder)
--- local result = valuebuilder.finish(builder)  -- {41}
--- ```
---
--- @param builder the stream
--- @return the one value the stream published
--- @raises string when a container is still open, or when no root was published
export const finish = implementation.finish
